      "nets": ["+5V", "GND", "servo power rail"],
      "observed": "D3’s output/lower terminal is wired to the servo power rail, and that rail is also wired directly to GND. The servo-header power pins and the capacitor-bank positive terminals therefore sit on GND.",
      "expected": "D3 should feed a dedicated, ungrounded servo power rail from +5V; the capacitors should connect between that rail and GND, and the servo-header power pins should receive the rail.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "D3 PMEG3030EP; its lower/output wire joins the servo rail and the GND symbol (sheet /, page 1)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "From +5V, a second diode D2 ... feeds a dedicated servo power rail backed by four 100 µF capacitors ... before reaching J3/J8 pin 2. (§4, Servo power buffering)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The servo supply is shorted to ground. Applying +5V can cause a large current through D3 and may pull down or damage the supply; the servos will not receive usable power.",
      "confidence": 0.97
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic connects the servo power rail after D3 directly to GND, contrary to the specified isolated, capacitor-buffered servo supply. This would short the rail and prevent normal servo operation."
}

